 class="m" font-size="8.5" font-weight="600" fill="var(--brand)">$74</text><line x1="10" y1="150" x2="360" y2="150" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1.2"></line><text x="50" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="m" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)">may</text><text x="122" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="m" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)">jun</text><text x="194" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="m" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)">jul</text><text x="266" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="m" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)">aug</text><text x="338" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="m" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)">sep</text></svg></div>
        <div class="body">
          <div class="t">Continuously optimize</div>
          <div class="b">We keep re-testing as models, quality, and prices change, so your workloads stay on the best fit without manual testing or ongoing tuning on your end.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="vblock sC">
      <div class="vhead"><span class="m vtag">C</span><div><div class="vname">The rail</div><div class="vnote">The numbers come out of the cards entirely and sit on a hairline above the row, so the eye reads one, two, three across before it reads any card. The line carries on past step three, which is the only one of the three that draws the sequence rather than labelling it.</div></div></div>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">How it works</h2>
      <div class="rail">
        <div class="rail-line"></div>
        <div class="rail-grid">
          <div class="rail-cell"><span class="rail-dot">1</span><span class="rail-lbl">Step 1</span></div>
          <div class="rail-cell"><span class="rail-dot">2</span><span class="rail-lbl">Step 2</span></div>
          <div class="rail-cell"><span class="rail-dot">3</span><span class="rail-lbl">Step 3</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="three">
      <div class="lp-card">
